my name is Sam Norman Haig nur and I'm a  postdoctoral associate in Nancy  kanwisher and Josh McDermott's lab at  MIT  my lab is interested in the overall  organization of the human brain what are  the parts of the brain and how do they  relate to parts of the mind that's why  I'm interested in this I want to know  who we are as thinkers what the basic  pieces are of the human mind and I think  we can see those by looking at the  organization of the brain so at present  some surprisingly little is known about  the basic organization and functional  properties of auditory cortex which is  the part of the brain that responds to  sounds been involved in processing music  and speech and other other complex  environmental sounds so there had been  many proposals for how human auditory  cortex might be organized into regions  and their association with function but  really very little consensus so the goal  of this study was to try to step back  and take a very broad view of how  humanity our cortex might be organized  and to not really be particularly  reliant on any high pop our hypothesis  about that organization  weas we measured responses using an MRI  in ten different human subjects so what  that means is that we put someone in the  scanner and we played them sounds while  we measured responses with an MRI  machine so in practice we measured  responses to 165 different sounds like  for example for sound of toilet flushing  or the sound of I'm so many cards or the  sound of someone walking they're just  sort of the sounds that we hear and in  everyday life and so the result of that  experiment is that basically we get a  matrix and what that matrix tells us as  it tells us for every point in the brain  how much of that point in the brain  respond to each of the hundred  sixty-five sounds what emerge from from  the analysis was that there's a  relatively small number of functional  properties that seem to dictate the  organization of auditory cortex when we  find that there's about six of these  canonical components that seem to be  able to account for the responses across  auditory cortex in different proportions  in different parts of the brain the  first four components that emerge from  this analysis were aspects of the neural  response to sound that we sort of  anticipated from other work but two of  the components that just popped out of  this very broad screen were striking  selectivity for speech sounds and  striking selectivity for music and that  really blew our minds  so that this result was surprising to us  for for several reasons  one is that when I first came to grad  school I spent basically a year or two  looking for brain regions that respond  selectively to music and basically  failed to find them so going into the  study I actually I I expected not to  find clear selectivity for music in the  explanation for why we were able to see  this clearly with with this method as  opposed to other methods including  methods that we had had previously used  is that that we think that the raw data  of FM rise is spatially coarse and as a  result you need to model the raw data  you need to model voxels as reflecting  multiple things in this case multiple  neural populations and that that  modeling procedure effectively allows  you to resolve patterns of selectivity  that are not able to be seen very  clearly in the raw data so it's it's  it's extremely interesting to to find  some evidence that in fact they're due  there does seem to be neural machinery  that is specialized to some extent for  music perception now what we still don't  really know is like where that comes  from is that something that humans are  born with is that something that emerges  over the course of development because  we get lots of exposure to music and  it's and it's an important and  interesting signal there's a lot of  really deep and open questions that I  think really speak to two fundamental  issues about where music comes from why  we have it and and how it evolved if it  did and so I think the ability to see  this this pup neural population  potentially gives us a method that we  can use to get some leverage on those  questions  you
 

